INSERT INTO cases (title, specialty, patient_age, patient_gender, chief_complaint, excerpt, history, examination, investigations, diagnosis, management, discussion, image_url, is_published, is_free) VALUES
('Routine Antenatal Care Visit', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Pregnancy, Screening, Routine', 'Every ANC visit is a screening opportunity — Pakistan''s maternal mortality remains high partly due to missed antenatal contacts.', 'Gestational age (LMP-based, confirm with early dating scan if available), gravida/para, obstetric history (previous complications, mode of delivery)
Current symptoms: fetal movements, bleeding, leaking, headache/visual disturbance, swelling
Medical history: diabetes, hypertension, anaemia, thyroid disease, previous caesarean
Social history: domestic support, nutrition, tetanus toxoid status, smoking/naswar use
Danger sign screening at every visit: bleeding, severe headache, reduced fetal movements, fever', 'Blood pressure and urine dipstick (protein) at every visit — essential pre-eclampsia screening
Symphysis-fundal height measurement, plot on growth chart
Fetal heart auscultation, presentation assessment in third trimester
Weight and BMI at booking; oedema check
Pallor check (anaemia is very common in Pakistani pregnant women)', 'Booking bloods: CBC, blood group & Rh, hepatitis B/C, HIV, syphilis (VDRL), rubella status if available
Urine routine examination and culture, urine protein at every visit
Fasting/OGTT for gestational diabetes screening (24–28 weeks)
Dating and anomaly ultrasound scans at appropriate gestations
Repeat CBC in third trimester to recheck for anaemia', NULL, 'Routine iron/folic acid and calcium supplementation throughout pregnancy
Screen and manage anaemia, hypertension, and gestational diabetes as they arise
Tetanus toxoid immunisation per schedule (2 doses if unimmunised, booster if previously vaccinated)
Individualised birth planning — discuss where and how the woman plans to deliver, encourage skilled birth attendance/facility delivery
Danger sign education at every visit: bleeding, reduced movements, severe headache, swelling, fever — when to seek urgent care immediately

PRESCRIPTION:
Ferrous Sulfate + Folic Acid Folisafe/Ferfolic/Obimin 1 tab OD throughout pregnancy. Routine supplementation per WHO for all pregnant women
Calcium Carbonate Calcimax/Ostocalcium 500mg-1g OD-BD from 20 weeks. WHO recommends for pre-eclampsia risk reduction, especially in low-calcium-intake populations
Tetanus Toxoid vaccine EPI supply (Tetanus Toxoid) 2 doses 4 weeks apart if unvaccinated, or booster per schedule. Prevents neonatal tetanus
Multivitamin (if additional micronutrient need) Obimin/Materna 1 tab OD. Adjunct to iron/folic acid/calcium, not a substitute', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
BP ≥140/90 with proteinuria, or severe headache/visual disturbance/epigastric pain → pre-eclampsia workup, urgent referral
Reduced/absent fetal movements → urgent fetal assessment (CTG/ultrasound)
Antepartum haemorrhage of any amount → urgent obstetric assessment, do NOT perform vaginal exam until placenta praevia excluded
Severe anaemia (Hb <7g/dl), signs of infection/fever, or reduced symphysis-fundal height (growth restriction concern)

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO Antenatal Care Guidelines (2016, ''positive pregnancy experience'' model) and NICE NG201: recommend a minimum of 8 antenatal contacts (WHO) across pregnancy; routine iron and folic acid supplementation for all pregnant women; screen and treat anaemia, hypertension, and gestational diabetes; tetanus toxoid vaccination per schedule; every contact should include BP/urine protein check, fetal growth/wellbeing assessment, and danger-sign counselling given Pakistan''s context of limited antenatal follow-through.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Attend all scheduled ANC visits, take iron/folic acid daily, immediate danger signs to watch for (bleeding, reduced fetal movement, severe headache/visual change, swelling of face/hands), plan for a skilled birth attendant/facility delivery.', NULL, true, false),
('Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Irregular cycles, Fertility, Metabolic', 'Irregular cycles plus acne and hair growth in a young woman — extremely common OPD presentation.', 'Menstrual irregularity (oligomenorrhoea/amenorrhoea), difficulty conceiving if trying
Hirsutism (facial/body hair growth), acne, hair thinning (androgenic pattern)
Weight gain, difficulty losing weight, acanthosis nigricans
Family history of PCOS, diabetes, or metabolic syndrome
Age at menarche, contraceptive history, psychological impact (anxiety/body image concerns)', 'BMI, waist circumference, blood pressure
Assess hirsutism using Ferriman-Gallwey score if formally required, acne distribution
Acanthosis nigricans (neck, axillae) — marker of insulin resistance
Pelvic exam not always necessary; can be omitted in straightforward presentation with typical history
Exclude other causes of hyperandrogenism if signs are rapidly progressive/severe (virilisation)', 'Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal preferred) for polycystic ovarian morphology
Hormonal profile: LH/FSH ratio, testosterone, SHBG, prolactin, TSH (to exclude other causes of irregular cycles)
Fasting glucose/OGTT and lipid profile (metabolic screening — PCOS carries higher diabetes/CVD risk)
Rule out pregnancy in any woman with amenorrhoea', NULL, 'Lifestyle modification: weight loss (even modest reduction improves ovulation and symptoms), regular exercise, dietary counselling
If not seeking pregnancy: combined oral contraceptive pill to regulate cycles and reduce hyperandrogenism/endometrial risk from chronic anovulation
If insulin resistance/metabolic features present: metformin as adjunct
If trying to conceive: letrozole or clomiphene as first-line ovulation induction (specialist-guided)
Address cosmetic concerns (hirsutism/acne) with topical/anti-androgen therapy where appropriate; psychological support for body image concerns

PRESCRIPTION:
Combined Oral Contraceptive Pill (if not seeking pregnancy) Diane-35 (also has anti-androgenic effect)/Yasmin/Femilon 1 tab OD for 21 days then 7-day break, or as directed. Regulates cycles, reduces hyperandrogenism/acne; avoid if contraindications to COCP (smoker >35, migraine with aura, VTE risk)
Metformin (for insulin resistance/metabolic features) Glucophage/Panformin 500 mg OD, titrate to BD-TDS. Take with food to reduce GI upset
Letrozole (first-line for ovulation induction if trying to conceive — specialist initiated) Femara 2.5-5mg OD days 3-7 of cycle. Requires monitoring, specialist/fertility clinic guidance', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Rapidly progressive virilisation (deepening voice, clitoromegaly, severe rapid-onset hirsutism) → suspect androgen-secreting tumour, urgent referral
Amenorrhoea with possibility of pregnancy — always exclude pregnancy first
Signs of Cushing''s syndrome (if suspected) need separate workup
Very irregular bleeding/heavy bleeding needs endometrial assessment given unopposed oestrogen risk with chronic anovulation

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per Rotterdam criteria (endorsed by ESHRE/ASRM international guideline) and NICE CKS: diagnosis requires 2 of 3 — oligo/anovulation, clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovaries on ultrasound — after excluding other causes; first-line management is lifestyle modification (weight loss even 5-10% improves symptoms/ovulation); combined oral contraceptive pill for cycle regulation and hyperandrogenism if not seeking pregnancy; metformin for insulin resistance/metabolic features; clomiphene/letrozole as first-line ovulation induction if trying to conceive.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Weight management is the single most effective intervention, regular follow-up for metabolic screening (glucose/lipids annually), discuss fertility plans openly to guide contraceptive vs ovulation-induction approach, address psychological impact of symptoms.', NULL, true, false),
('Abnormal Uterine Bleeding / Menorrhagia', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy periods, Anaemia, Common', 'Heavy periods disrupting daily life — a leading cause of iron deficiency anaemia in Pakistani women.', 'Cycle pattern: frequency, duration, heaviness (number of pads/day, flooding, passing clots, impact on daily activities)
Intermenstrual or postcoital bleeding (concerning features needing further workup)
Associated symptoms: dysmenorrhoea, pelvic pain/pressure (suggests fibroids/adenomyosis)
Contraceptive use (IUCD can cause heavier bleeding), bleeding disorders/family history
Age — perimenopausal bleeding changes need endometrial assessment; postmenopausal bleeding always needs urgent workup', 'General: pallor (anaemia), BMI, thyroid exam (if hypothyroidism suspected as a cause)
Abdominal exam for palpable masses (large fibroids)
Speculum exam to visualise cervix, exclude local cervical pathology/polyps
Bimanual pelvic exam to assess uterine size/mobility/tenderness, adnexal masses', 'CBC (assess anaemia severity)
Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal preferred) to assess endometrial thickness, fibroids, adnexal pathology
TSH (hypothyroidism is a treatable cause of menorrhagia)
Coagulation screen if history suggests bleeding disorder (especially in adolescents with heavy bleeding since menarche)
Endometrial biopsy if age >45, risk factors for endometrial cancer, or ultrasound shows thickened endometrium', NULL, 'Correct anaemia first with iron replacement if significant
LNG-IUS (hormonal coil) is first-line per NICE if no fertility desire in the near term and no structural contraindication
Tranexamic acid or NSAIDs (mefenamic acid) as non-hormonal options, particularly if trying to conceive
Combined oral contraceptive pill as an alternative hormonal option
Investigate and treat underlying structural cause (fibroids, polyps) if identified; refer for surgical options if medical management fails

PRESCRIPTION:
Tranexamic Acid Transamin/Cyklokapron 1g TDS during heavy bleeding days (max 4g/day), only during menses. Non-hormonal option, good if trying to conceive
Mefenamic Acid (NSAID) Ponstan 500 mg TDS during menses. Also helps with dysmenorrhoea; avoid in peptic ulcer disease
Levonorgestrel IUS (first-line per NICE) Mirena Inserted by trained provider, effective up to 5 years. Most effective medical option; requires insertion procedure
Ferrous Sulfate (if anaemic) Ferrous Sulphate/Fefol 200 mg OD-BD. Continue for 3 months after Hb normalises', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Postmenopausal bleeding → always urgent endometrial cancer pathway referral (transvaginal ultrasound/biopsy)
Intermenstrual/postcoital bleeding, or abnormal-looking cervix → urgent gynae referral to exclude cervical/endometrial pathology
Severe anaemia (Hb <7g/dl) or haemodynamic instability from acute heavy bleeding → urgent management, possible transfusion
Rapidly enlarging pelvic mass

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG88 (Heavy menstrual bleeding): first-line pharmacological treatment (if no structural/histological abnormality, or fibroids <3cm) is the levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS); alternative options include tranexamic acid or NSAIDs (non-hormonal, for those wanting to conceive) or combined hormonal contraception; surgical options (endometrial ablation, hysterectomy) considered if medical management fails or structural cause found; investigate with ultrasound first-line rather than proceeding straight to hysteroscopy in most cases.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Track bleeding pattern (pad count/duration) to monitor response, correct iron deficiency alongside treating the bleeding, discuss fertility plans to guide choice between hormonal/non-hormonal options, seek urgent review for postmenopausal or intermenstrual bleeding.', NULL, true, false),
('Vaginal Discharge / Vaginitis', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Discharge, Infection, Common', 'One of the most common — and most stigmatised — gynae OPD complaints in Pakistan; handle with sensitivity.', 'Discharge character: colour, odour, consistency (thin/watery vs thick/curdy), amount
Associated itching/burning (suggests candidiasis), fishy odour especially after intercourse (suggests bacterial vaginosis), frothy discharge (trichomoniasis)
Sexual history if appropriate/comfortable to discuss, partner symptoms
Diabetes (recurrent candidiasis association), recent antibiotic use, contraceptive method (IUCD)
Pelvic pain, fever, dyspareunia — concerning for pelvic inflammatory disease', 'Speculum examination to visualise discharge character and cervix (look for cervicitis, cervical motion tenderness)
Assess for vulvar erythema/excoriation (candidiasis)
Bimanual exam if pelvic pain present — check for cervical motion tenderness, adnexal tenderness (PID)
Check for fever, lower abdominal tenderness', 'High vaginal swab microscopy/culture (clue cells for BV, hyphae for candida, motile trichomonads)
Vaginal pH testing if available (BV/trichomoniasis pH >4.5, candidiasis typically normal pH)
Screen for other STIs if sexually transmitted infection suspected/risk factors present
Blood glucose if recurrent candidiasis (exclude undiagnosed diabetes)', NULL, 'Bacterial vaginosis: oral or vaginal metronidazole
Candidiasis: topical clotrimazole pessary/cream or oral fluconazole; address predisposing factors (diabetes control, avoid tight synthetic underwear)
Trichomoniasis: oral metronidazole for patient AND partner (true STI, partner treatment essential)
PID: combination antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole), partner treatment, and follow-up review
General advice: cotton underwear, avoid douching/harsh soaps, good perineal hygiene

PRESCRIPTION:
Metronidazole (for BV/Trichomoniasis) Flagyl 400-500mg BD for 7 days (or 2g single dose for trichomoniasis). Avoid alcohol during and 48h after treatment; treat partner for trichomoniasis
Clotrimazole vaginal pessary (for candidiasis) Candid-V/Canesten 500mg single pessary intravaginally, or 100mg OD for 6 nights. Topical option, effective and well-tolerated
Fluconazole (oral alternative for candidiasis) Diflucan/Flucos 150 mg single oral dose. Convenient single-dose alternative to topical treatment
Ceftriaxone + Doxycycline + Metronidazole (if PID) Rocephin + Doxin + Flagyl Ceftriaxone 500mg-1g IM once + Doxycycline 100mg BD 14 days + Metronidazole 400mg BD 14 days. Full PID regimen; ensure partner is also treated', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Fever, severe pelvic pain, cervical motion tenderness → suspect pelvic inflammatory disease, needs prompt antibiotic treatment and partner treatment
Postmenopausal bleeding with discharge → exclude malignancy
Recurrent/persistent symptoms despite treatment → consider resistant infection, diabetes screening, or alternative diagnosis
Pregnancy with abnormal discharge — different management thresholds, refer as needed

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO STI management guidelines and NICE CKS: bacterial vaginosis treated with metronidazole (oral or vaginal); candidiasis treated with topical or oral azole antifungals; trichomoniasis (a true STI) requires metronidazole for the patient AND partner treatment; pelvic inflammatory disease requires broad-spectrum antibiotic combination covering gonorrhoea, chlamydia, and anaerobes, plus partner notification and treatment.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Complete full treatment course, avoid intercourse until treatment complete (especially for STIs), partner treatment where relevant, cotton underwear and good hygiene, avoid vaginal douching, return if symptoms persist or recur frequently.', NULL, true, false),
('Anaemia in Pregnancy', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Pregnancy, Anaemia, Iron', 'Extremely prevalent in Pakistani pregnant women — a major contributor to maternal morbidity if untreated.', 'Fatigue, breathlessness, palpitations, dizziness — may be attributed to ''normal pregnancy tiredness'' and missed
Dietary intake of iron-rich foods, pica, multiple/closely spaced pregnancies
Pre-pregnancy history of anaemia, menorrhagia before pregnancy
Compliance with antenatal iron supplementation so far
Symptoms of severe anaemia: breathlessness at rest, chest pain, ankle swelling (high-output cardiac strain)', 'Pallor of conjunctiva, palms, nail beds
Tachycardia, flow murmur if significant anaemia
Signs of cardiac decompensation if severe (raised JVP, basal crackles, oedema) — obstetric emergency if present
Fundal height/fetal assessment (severe anaemia can affect fetal growth)', 'CBC with red cell indices — note: microcytic picture may indicate thalassaemia trait (common in Pakistan) rather than pure iron deficiency
Serum ferritin to confirm iron deficiency
Haemoglobin electrophoresis if thalassaemia trait suspected (family history, disproportionately low MCV for degree of anaemia)
Stool for occult blood/ova & parasites if indicated
Repeat CBC after treatment to confirm response', NULL, 'Oral iron and folic acid supplementation (higher therapeutic dose than routine prophylactic dose if anaemia confirmed)
Dietary counselling: iron-rich foods, vitamin C co-administration to enhance absorption, separate iron dosing from tea/calcium intake
Recheck Hb after 2-4 weeks to confirm response; if inadequate, consider IV iron or investigate for thalassaemia/other causes
IV iron if oral intolerance, malabsorption, or limited time before delivery to correct orally
Screen partner/consider haemoglobin electrophoresis if thalassaemia trait suspected, given its prevalence in Pakistan — genetic counselling implications for future pregnancies

PRESCRIPTION:
Ferrous Sulfate + Folic Acid (therapeutic dose) Ferrous Sulphate/Folisafe 200mg BD-TDS (higher than prophylactic OD dose) with folic acid 5mg OD. Take with vitamin C source; separate from tea/calcium by 2 hours
IV Iron Sucrose (if oral not tolerated/severe/late gestation) Venofer Weight/deficit-based calculation, given as infusion. Hospital/clinic administered, faster correction than oral
Folic Acid (standalone if separate from combination) Folic acid 5mg tablets 5mg OD. Important alongside iron, especially if any suspicion of coexisting folate deficiency', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Hb <7g/dl (severe anaemia) → high risk for both mother and fetus, needs urgent correction (may need transfusion), obstetric-led care
Symptoms/signs of cardiac decompensation (breathlessness at rest, tachycardia, oedema) → emergency, admit
Anaemia not responding to oral iron after 2-4 weeks → investigate for other causes (thalassaemia trait very relevant in Pakistan, malabsorption, ongoing blood loss)
Antepartum haemorrhage superimposed on anaemia — compounds risk significantly

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO guidance on antenatal anaemia and NICE NG201: all pregnant women should receive routine oral iron and folic acid supplementation; screen with CBC at booking and again in third trimester; oral iron is first-line for mild-moderate iron deficiency anaemia; parenteral (IV) iron considered if oral is not tolerated, malabsorption present, or anaemia is severe/late in pregnancy with limited time for oral correction; blood transfusion reserved for severe anaemia with cardiac compromise or near term with active bleeding risk.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Continue iron throughout pregnancy and into postpartum period, take with vitamin C-rich food, avoid tea/coffee close to dosing, attend follow-up blood tests to confirm response, discuss birth planning given anaemia may affect delivery risk if uncorrected.', NULL, true, false),
('Pre-eclampsia', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Pregnancy, Emergency, Hypertension', 'New hypertension plus proteinuria after 20 weeks — a leading cause of maternal death if missed.', 'Headache (often frontal, persistent, not relieved by usual analgesia), visual disturbance (blurring, flashing lights), epigastric/right upper quadrant pain
Swelling of face/hands (beyond usual ankle swelling of pregnancy), sudden significant weight gain
Reduced urine output, reduced fetal movements
Risk factors: first pregnancy, previous pre-eclampsia, chronic hypertension, diabetes, multiple pregnancy, obesity, age >40, family history
Gestational age — classically after 20 weeks gestation', 'Blood pressure measurement (repeat/confirm if elevated), urine dipstick for protein
Assess for oedema (face, hands, sudden onset, not just dependent ankle swelling)
Reflexes — hyperreflexia/clonus suggests impending eclampsia
Epigastric/RUQ tenderness (suggests liver involvement, HELLP syndrome)
Fundal height and fetal heart assessment', 'Blood pressure and urine protein:creatinine ratio or dipstick (confirm significant proteinuria)
FBC (platelets — thrombocytopenia in severe disease/HELLP), LFTs (elevated transaminases), renal function (creatinine, uric acid)
Coagulation profile if severe/HELLP suspected
CTG and ultrasound (growth, liquor, umbilical artery Doppler) for fetal wellbeing assessment', NULL, 'Any suspected pre-eclampsia needs same-day obstetric assessment — this is not a case to manage purely in general OPD
Antihypertensives to control severe BP (labetalol first-line in pregnancy) while arranging transfer
Magnesium sulphate for eclampsia prophylaxis in severe disease or treatment of an eclamptic seizure (specialist/inpatient setting)
Corticosteroids (dexamethasone/betamethasone) if preterm delivery anticipated, for fetal lung maturation
Definitive management is timed delivery — balance of maternal and fetal risk, decided by the obstetric team

PRESCRIPTION:
Labetalol (first-line antihypertensive in pregnancy) Trandate 100-200mg BD-TDS oral, or IV in severe/emergency setting. First-line per NICE for pregnancy hypertension; avoid in asthma
Nifedipine (alternative first-line) Adalat Retard 10-20mg BD (modified release). Alternative to labetalol, avoid sublingual immediate-release forms
Magnesium Sulphate (severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia — inpatient only) MgSO4 injection Per Pritchard/Zuspan protocol, loading then maintenance IV/IM. Requires inpatient monitoring (reflexes, urine output, respiratory rate) for toxicity
Dexamethasone (if preterm delivery anticipated) Decadron 12mg IM, 2 doses 24h apart. For fetal lung maturity if <34-36 weeks and delivery anticipated', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
BP ≥160/110 (severe hypertension) → emergency, admit immediately regardless of symptoms
Severe headache, visual disturbance, epigastric pain, hyperreflexia/clonus → impending eclampsia, emergency admission
Seizure (eclampsia) → obstetric/medical emergency, magnesium sulphate and urgent delivery planning
Signs of HELLP syndrome (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) or reduced urine output/fetal movements → urgent same-day obstetric assessment

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG133 (Hypertension in pregnancy) and WHO pre-eclampsia guidelines: any BP ≥140/90 after 20 weeks with proteinuria requires prompt specialist assessment; oral labetalol is first-line antihypertensive in pregnancy (nifedipine or methyldopa as alternatives); magnesium sulphate is used for seizure prophylaxis in severe pre-eclampsia and treatment of eclampsia; definitive treatment is delivery of the placenta — timing balances maternal risk against fetal maturity, with corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity if preterm delivery anticipated.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
This diagnosis requires same-day referral to obstetric care — do not manage expectantly in a general OPD setting. Educate all antenatal patients to report headache, visual changes, epigastric pain, or swelling immediately rather than waiting for the next scheduled visit.', NULL, true, false),
('Dysmenorrhoea (Painful Periods)', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Pelvic pain, Menstrual, Young women', 'Cramping pain that sends young women to the OPD every month — usually primary, but always screen for secondary causes.', 'Onset relative to menarche (primary dysmenorrhoea typically starts within 1-2 years of menarche) vs new onset later in life (suggests secondary cause)
Pain timing: primary dysmenorrhoea starts just before/with onset of bleeding, cramping, lower abdominal, may radiate to back/thighs
Associated symptoms suggesting secondary causes: deep dyspareunia, chronic pelvic pain outside menses, heavy bleeding, infertility (suggests endometriosis/adenomyosis/fibroids)
Impact on daily activities/school-work attendance
Response to previous analgesics if tried', 'Abdominal exam — usually unremarkable in primary dysmenorrhoea
Pelvic exam (if appropriate/consented, may be deferred in young unmarried patients per cultural context) — look for tenderness, fixed retroverted uterus, nodularity (suggestive of endometriosis) in secondary causes
Assess for signs of fibroids (enlarged uterus) if secondary cause suspected
General assessment for anaemia if associated heavy bleeding', 'Primary dysmenorrhoea in a young patient with typical history and normal exam: usually no investigations needed, clinical diagnosis
Pelvic ultrasound if secondary cause suspected (fibroids, endometrioma, adenomyosis features)
CBC if heavy bleeding is also present
Diagnostic laparoscopy (specialist-referred) if endometriosis strongly suspected and not responding to treatment', NULL, 'First-line: NSAIDs started at onset of pain or 1-2 days before expected period, continued through the painful days
Combined oral contraceptive pill if contraception also wanted or NSAIDs alone insufficient
Heat application (hot water bottle) as adjunct, regular exercise may help
If secondary dysmenorrhoea suspected — investigate and treat underlying cause (e.g. laparoscopy/hormonal treatment for endometriosis)
Reassurance and normalisation while ensuring secondary causes aren''t missed, especially if pain is atypical or progressive

PRESCRIPTION:
Mefenamic Acid (NSAID) Ponstan 500mg TDS, started at pain onset or 1-2 days before period, continued 2-3 days. First-line; take with food, avoid in peptic ulcer disease
Ibuprofen (alternative NSAID) Brufen 400mg TDS PRN during painful days. Alternative NSAID option
Combined Oral Contraceptive Pill (if also wants contraception/NSAIDs insufficient) Yasmin/Femilon/Diane-35 1 tab OD as per packet instructions. Second-line/adjunct, also regulates cycle', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
New onset severe pain after previously pain-free cycles, especially with dyspareunia or subfertility → investigate for endometriosis/adenomyosis
Pain not responding to standard first-line treatment → reassess for secondary cause
Associated abnormal bleeding pattern or pelvic mass → further gynae workup needed
Severe acute pain with fever → exclude PID or other acute gynae/surgical emergency

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS and ACOG guidance: first-line treatment for primary dysmenorrhoea is NSAIDs (which inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, the underlying mechanism of pain) — more effective than paracetamol alone; combined oral contraceptive pill is an effective second-line/adjunct option, particularly if contraception also desired; secondary dysmenorrhoea (new onset, associated with other symptoms) warrants investigation for underlying pathology (endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, PID) rather than symptomatic treatment alone.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Start the NSAID a day or two before expected pain onset for best effect, use a heat pack for additional relief, track cycle pattern, seek review if pain is new, worsening, or not responding to first-line treatment — this may indicate a secondary cause needing further workup.', NULL, true, false),
('Menopause', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Midlife, Hormonal, Hot flushes', 'Hot flushes and mood changes bringing women to OPD, often unsure whether what they''re feeling is ''normal''.', 'Menstrual pattern change/cessation — menopause confirmed retrospectively after 12 months of amenorrhoea
Vasomotor symptoms: hot flushes, night sweats; psychological symptoms: mood changes, anxiety, low mood, sleep disturbance
Urogenital symptoms: vaginal dryness, dyspareunia, urinary frequency/urgency
Impact on quality of life, work, relationships — often under-discussed culturally and worth asking about directly
Personal/family history of breast cancer, VTE, cardiovascular disease, osteoporosis risk factors (relevant to HRT decision-making)', 'Blood pressure, BMI
General examination — most menopausal diagnosis is clinical based on age and symptom pattern
Pelvic exam if abnormal bleeding or urogenital symptoms need assessment
Breast examination/discussion of screening mammography if age-appropriate
Assess for signs of osteoporosis risk (height loss, kyphosis) if long-standing', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis in women >45 with typical symptoms — hormone testing not routinely needed
FSH level may help in women <45 or with atypical presentation (raised FSH supports diagnosis) or after hysterectomy without oophorectomy
Bone density (DEXA) scan if osteoporosis risk factors present
Lipid profile and cardiovascular risk assessment given increased risk post-menopause
Transvaginal ultrasound/endometrial biopsy if any postmenopausal bleeding', NULL, 'Discuss and offer HRT for bothersome vasomotor/psychological symptoms after individualised risk-benefit discussion (personal/family history of breast cancer, VTE, cardiovascular disease)
Vaginal oestrogen (low systemic absorption) specifically for urogenital symptoms — can be used even alongside or instead of systemic HRT
Non-hormonal options (SSRIs/SNRIs, clonidine, lifestyle measures — cooling strategies, exercise, weight management) if HRT declined or contraindicated
Bone health: calcium/vitamin D adequacy, weight-bearing exercise, DEXA scan if risk factors present
Psychological support and open discussion — menopause remains under-discussed in many Pakistani households, and validating symptoms matters

PRESCRIPTION:
Combined HRT (oestrogen + progestogen, if uterus intact) Femoston/Cliogest As per product regimen, individualised. Requires informed discussion of risks/benefits; not for women with active breast cancer/unexplained bleeding
Vaginal Oestrogen (for isolated urogenital symptoms) Ovestin cream/pessary Nightly for 2 weeks then 2x/week maintenance. Minimal systemic absorption, can be used long-term for local symptoms
Venlafaxine (non-hormonal option for vasomotor symptoms) Efexor 37.5-75mg OD. Alternative if HRT declined/contraindicated
Calcium + Vitamin D (bone health) Calcimax/Ostocalcium 500-1200mg calcium + 800IU vitamin D daily. Supports bone health through and after the menopausal transition', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Postmenopausal bleeding (any bleeding after 12 months of amenorrhoea) → always urgent referral to exclude endometrial pathology, regardless of how minor
Menopause before age 40 (premature ovarian insufficiency) → needs specific hormonal workup and long-term bone/cardiovascular risk counselling
New severe mood symptoms/thoughts of self-harm → assess mental health urgently alongside menopause management
Unexplained weight loss or other systemic symptoms alongside menopausal symptoms → investigate separately

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG23 (Menopause: diagnosis and management): diagnose menopause clinically based on symptoms in women over 45 without needing hormone tests; offer HRT as first-line for vasomotor and other menopausal symptoms after discussing individualised risks/benefits (VTE, breast cancer, cardiovascular risk vary by HRT type/route and patient factors); vaginal oestrogen is effective and has minimal systemic absorption for isolated urogenital symptoms; non-hormonal options (SSRIs/SNRIs, lifestyle measures) are appropriate alternatives for those who cannot/prefer not to use HRT.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Menopausal symptoms are common and very treatable — encourage open discussion rather than ''just coping''; any bleeding after 12 months without a period needs prompt review, not reassurance; discuss bone and heart health proactively as part of long-term care.', NULL, true, false),
('Uterine Fibroids', 'Gynae/Obs', NULL, NULL, 'Pelvic mass, Heavy periods, Benign', 'Heavy periods and a ''lump'' feeling in the pelvis — among the most common benign gynae findings in women over 30.', 'Heavy and/or prolonged menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure/fullness, urinary frequency (bladder pressure) or constipation (rectal pressure) from mass effect
Dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia, subfertility or recurrent pregnancy loss in some cases
Rate of growth, associated pain (rapid growth or severe pain can suggest red degeneration, especially in pregnancy)
Family history of fibroids, age (more common approaching perimenopause), parity
Impact on quality of life and any fertility plans (important for guiding management choice)', 'Abdominal exam — palpable pelvic mass if fibroids are large, may feel irregular/nodular (''bosselated'' uterus)
Bimanual pelvic exam — enlarged, irregular, mobile uterus typically
Assess for pallor if associated heavy bleeding causing anaemia
Assess size in relation to gestational-equivalent weeks (a common way fibroid uteri are described clinically)', 'Pelvic ultrasound (transabdominal/transvaginal) — confirms number, size, and location of fibroids
CBC to assess for anaemia from heavy bleeding
MRI pelvis if planning surgical/interventional treatment (better anatomical mapping) or diagnosis unclear
Endometrial biopsy if abnormal bleeding pattern raises concern for coexisting endometrial pathology', NULL, 'Asymptomatic small fibroids: reassurance and routine follow-up, no treatment needed
Symptomatic with heavy bleeding: LNG-IUS (if cavity not significantly distorted), tranexamic acid, or combined hormonal contraception as medical options
GnRH agonists can be used short-term pre-operatively to shrink fibroids and correct anaemia before surgery
Myomectomy for women wishing to preserve fertility with symptomatic fibroids
Uterine artery embolisation or hysterectomy considered for those who have completed childbearing with significant symptoms not responding to medical management

PRESCRIPTION:
Tranexamic Acid (for heavy bleeding) Transamin 1g TDS during heavy bleeding days. Non-hormonal option, useful adjunct while planning definitive treatment
