Nitrofurantoin (safe in 1st/2nd trimester, avoid near term) Furadantin/Macrobid 100mg BD for 7 days. Avoid after 36 weeks (theoretical neonatal haemolysis risk)
Amoxicillin/Cephalexin (if culture-sensitive, safe throughout pregnancy) Amoxil/Keflex 500mg TDS for 7 days. Choose based on culture sensitivity; generally safe throughout pregnancy
Co-amoxiclav or IV Ceftriaxone (for pyelonephritis, inpatient) Augmentin/Rocephin 625mg TDS oral or 1g IV OD, per severity, 10-14 days total. Admit for IV therapy initially if systemically unwell
Avoid: Trimethoprim in 1st trimester; Ciprofloxacin throughout pregnancy — — —. Trimethoprim is a folate antagonist (neural tube risk in 1st trimester); fluoroquinolones avoided due to cartilage/joint concerns', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Fever, rigors, loin pain, vomiting → pyelonephritis in pregnancy, admit for IV antibiotics and monitoring (maternal and fetal)
Signs of preterm labour (regular contractions, cervical change) alongside UTI symptoms → urgent obstetric assessment
Sepsis signs (tachycardia, hypotension, confusion) → emergency management, admit immediately
Recurrent UTIs in pregnancy → consider prophylactic antibiotics and further urological assessment postpartum

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG109 and RCOG guidance: ALL pregnant women with bacteriuria (symptomatic or asymptomatic on screening) should be treated with antibiotics, since untreated bacteriuria in pregnancy significantly increases risk of pyelonephritis and preterm birth; antibiotic choice must consider pregnancy safety — nitrofurantoin is generally avoided near term (theoretical neonatal haemolysis risk), and trimethoprim is avoided in the first trimester (folate antagonist); a repeat urine culture after treatment (''test of cure'') is recommended to confirm clearance.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Complete the full antibiotic course even though pregnancy makes this feel like ''just another symptom'' — treatment reduces real risks of kidney infection and preterm labour; attend the repeat urine test after treatment to confirm the infection has fully cleared.', NULL, true, false);
